<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="reading-question-cross-products" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Cross products
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The <term> cross product </term> <m> { \times } \colon \of{ \R^{ 3 } }^{ 2 } \to \R^{ 3 } </m> is the binary operation on <m> 3 </m>-dimensional real coordinate space
      
      <md>
        \R^{ 3 } = \setmid{ \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } }{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in \R }
      </md>
      
      defined by the formula
      
      <md>
        \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } \times \of{ y_{ 1 } , y_{ 2 } , y_{ 3 } } = \of{ x_{ 2 } y_{ 3 } - x_{ 3 } y_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } y_{ 1 } - x_{ 1 } y_{ 3 } , x_{ 1 } y_{ 2 } - x_{ 2 } y_{ 1 } }
      </md>
      
      For this problem, you may assume all standard properties of the arithmetic of real numbers, even though we will not formally define <m> \R </m> until <xref ref="section-constructing-the-real-numbers"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether or not the cross product <m> \times </m> on <m> \R^{ 3 } </m> is commutative. Justify your answer.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Consider the vectors <m> \vect{ e }_{ 1 } = \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 } </m>, <m> \vect{ e }_{ 2 } = \of{ 0 , 1 , 0 } </m>, and <m> \vect{ e }_{ 3 } = \of{ 0 , 0 , 1 } </m>. We note that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 2 } \amp = \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 } \times \of{ 0 , 1 , 0 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ 0 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 1 , 0 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 0 , 1 \cdot 1 - 0 \cdot 0 } = \of{ 0 , 0 , 1 } = \vect{ e }_{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \vect{ e }_{ 2 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \amp = \of{ 0 , 1 , 0 } \times \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ 1 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 0 , 0 \cdot 1 - 0 \cdot 0 , 0 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 1 } = \of{ 0 , 0 , -1 } = -\vect{ e }_{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Since
        
        <md>
          \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 2 } = \vect{ e }_{ 3 } \neq -\vect{ e }_{ 3 } = \vect{ e }_{ 2 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 1 }
        </md>,
        
        the cross product <m> \times </m> is non-commutative.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether or not the cross product <m> \times </m> on <m> \R^{ 3 } </m> is associative. Justify your answer.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Consider the vectors <m> \vect{ e }_{ 1 } = \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 } </m>, <m> \vect{ e }_{ 2 } = \of{ 0 , 1 , 0 } </m>, and <m> \vect{ e }_{ 3 } = \of{ 0 , 0 , 1 } </m>. We note that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \amp = \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 } \times \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ 0 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 0 , 0 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 0 , 1 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 1 } = \of{ 0 , 0 , 0 } = \vect{ 0 }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \vect{ 0 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 2 } \amp = \of{ 0 , 0 , 0 } \times \of{ 0 , 1 , 0 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ 0 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 1 , 0 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 0 , 0 \cdot 1 - 0 \cdot 0 } = \of{ 0 , 0 , 0 } = \vect{ 0 }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 3 } \amp = \of{ 1 , 0 , 0 } \times \of{ 0 , 0 , 1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ 0 \cdot 1 - 0 \cdot 0 , 0 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 1 , 1 \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot 0 } = \of{ 0 , -1 , 0 } = -\vect{ e }_{ 2 }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Together with the computations above in (a), we see that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \of{ \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 1 } } \times \vect{ e }_{ 2 } \amp = \vect{ 0 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 2 } = \vect{ 0 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = -\vect{ e }_{ 2 } = \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 3 } = \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \of{ \vect{ e }_{ 1 } \times \vect{ e }_{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so the cross product <m> \times </m> is non-associative.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
